    "3:א": "(1) Ater Notel etc. per the column he wrote, even if he is right-handed, every person who is right-handed takes the etrog on the left, etc., that other judges have written on the right and left Didi' Azlinan, and I am puzzled that the column itself is written in Ramzi's book, and at the very least, the HL is to bring the opinion of the Rasht according to his way, and that KL Dam will take the Lulav on the left, Didi', how can he dispute the Lulav on the left? Didi, that is, the right of the whole world, and it seems to be simple, Dishh TS in the column and Tzal Yatul HaGoud on the left, every person, and G. Duaf, if he is ater, it is correct. DKML is a model in this, they follow the right, Didi. Later, I found that Rashel's proof of the words of the column and the kon is the same as what I came up with. And I was puzzled about the MS in clothing that is not practiced in the 11th and Rama already testified about the custom to the contrary, therefore one should not watch over someone who errs and behaves to the contrary:",
    "3:ב": "(2) And if the opposite happened. P. that if the reverse of the Hater and took the etrog on the left, every person went out, and per the Lad, it is not necessary to write this thing Dafi' every person who reversed and gave the etrog in our days and the association of Lulav on his left came out in hindsight, the main reason is because the lulav has three mitzvos in it, and that is the beginning, but in retrospect, it must have come out if the opposite is true, the mitzva exists and it is not written in the Torah, he took the lulav on the right, and this is proven In Gm. Defarchi' in S.P. and from Dagbia, we issued Biya and from two of my fathers when they turned, etc., and he did not say that Demiri took his left hand and likewise the judges who wrote before the blessing would make it worthwhile' Ahaz and they did not take it, we put it to Lulev on his left, but Psheita Dam, the opposite came out later, and so the Rama did not have to write here, and if the opposite came out, it means precisely in Iter, since there are opinions here and there, but in a different person It didn't come out if he didn't do as he went about it to begin with, and it's not like that except as a simple as above:",
    "5:_": "Before taking the ethrog. per the the Omnipresent and the Rashidun on behalf of Dain to bless before we cast it, it is not permissible to bless while it is placed in a suitable vessel in tefillin and it is not permissible to bless after he has taken any kind of divination. In fact, but in the opposite way, we did not issue the first and the first letter of the taking of the etrog, he goes to do it because the 4th species inhibits the ZAZ, because it does not come out if he wants to take A of them or Efi, C alone, because they are all one mitzvah, but if he wants to take them all in this Ahzz S4 Kmash in the Sib and here they did not come but L. Dahoi goes about doing them because the main mitzvah is still before him and there are those who bother In this case, he mentioned only two ways and not the third way, i.e., that the possibility of not going out seems benign. It is the third way, like the first two ways. per the third way, there is no point here in reminding him that there is a possibility in his heart that it is, and that there is no idea in his heart that it is true. In the first two ways, he has the feeling of reminding him that it has not yet come out, since you need to turn or take it before taking the etrog and stop between the adhesives, which are the first commandment and something that the Omnipresent has changed. A person who forgets Kana Lad:",
    "7:א": "If he wrapped cloth on it etc. — Tosafot 37b dispute Rabbah and Rava on taking through another object: they wrote that requiring complete taking here does not apply to interposition as above when binding in lulav; here when wrapping on the hand there is no interposition; one who wraps on his hand and takes is valid; case: he made a handle from cloth and the entire lulav is outside his hand and he holds the handle protruding from the cloth — Rava considers complete taking and no interposition; they challenged and say: vessel case — hand under edges or holding vessel handle — see their words' conclusion: lulav taken in vessel, not holding vessel walls but placing hand under edges or holding vessel handle — meaning handle from cloth helps holding lulav like tongs; whether lulav partly in hand or entirely outside, holding via cloth assistance is permitted as handle; therefore Tur and taking through another object is called taking — even if entire lulav outside hand provided honor path; if not honor path such as putting in vessel without need to hold lulav — invalid; Tur did not distinguish holding vessel walls vs edges because already wrote only honor path; no permission unless like tongs; if wrapped hand in cloth and took lulav — forbidden interposition; Yissachar of Kfar Barkai punished for this — Tosafot needed not force first explanation; explicit in chapter they brought him and chapter where practiced — all clear per Taz; Beit Yosef explained Tosafot differently — Taz did not descend to truth of Tosafot words.",
    "7:ב": "(2) And the whole hand is not covered. 22 II in the name of Aguda P. MS. And I don't know the source of this, an emphasis in the Lulav. Indeed, in all the hands, it was adraba from the words of the Rabbi who brought it in 2 from the meaning of Dahua as baptism and tefillin, and that Daha. For properness, there is no khitza, which means a thing that is not proper, he is a khitza, as for the rabbi and A'l Maddanan Rama Ogdin, the lulav with a thread or with an anointing, it is for his worthiness, and as the Torah, it is due to it because of a khitza, and a'l to Rabbi DSL Efi' for his worthiness, he is a khitza, for the Rabbi was the union, except for a place that holds the lovev, as said by Raba Lahanu Magdali Hoshana, the remnants of Beit Yad 20:",
